  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gaza Health Emergency Response Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Project aims to provide emergency support for the provision of essential health services to the population of Gaza. The Project will support activities aiming at ensuring continuity of essential health services in Gaza through the following components: Component 1 (Delivery of essential maternal, newborn, and child health (MNCH) services and communicable disease treatment) aims at maintaining vital maternal, newborn, and child health services in Gaza. It involves the provision of drugs, medical supplies, equipment, water, and other necessities for delivering medical services in primary, secondary, and tertiary governmental, non-governmental, and private health facilities. Additionally, this component may involve minor civil works to ensure continuity of services in damagedfacilities. Component 2 (Supporting provision of emergency care and treatment of chronic conditions) involves the provision of goods, including drugs, medical supplies, equipment, fuel, water, and other necessities, with the aim of ensuring the provision of emergency and trauma care, laboratory and diagnostic services, and priority interventions for non-communicable diseases such as hypertension and diabetes. Component 3 (Project coordination and management) will support the implementing agencies cost recovery and the costs associated with the third-party monitoring agency. This will include costs of the project implementation and coordination, as well as ensuring monitoring and evaluation (M&amp;amp;E) through hiring of a TPM agency Paragraph 12 of Section III of the IPF Policy (Projects in Situations of Urgent Need of Assistance or Capacity Constraints) is being applied to the preparation of the proposed Project. The grant recipients will be the United Nations Children&apos;s Fund (UNICEF), as the implementing agency for Component 1 and the World Health Organization (WHO), as the implementing agency for Component 2. Additionally, both agencies will work in coordination with various stakeholders within the health sector. Procurement will be carried out by both UN agencies in accordance with the Alternative Procurement Arrangements (APA) provisions approved by the Board of the Bank in July 2015. The Financial Management arrangements will follow the framework that is agreed to by a number of UN agencies who are signatories to the Financial Management Framework Agreement dated March 10, 2006, between the Bank, the International Development Association and the United Nations (FMFA).</narrative>
  </description>
